WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.580
Hello there. Now you may be wondering

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.400
what is so fast about a camera.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.310
Well I'll show you.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:11.143
When you take a picture

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:12.860
with a regular viewfinder camera,

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.200
you're not actually looking down

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.033
the same lens

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:15.866
that's taking the picture,

00:00:15.866 --> 00:00:16.699
you're looking down

00:00:16.699 --> 00:00:17.980
a separate viewfinder lens.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:18.990
And in some situations,

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:19.823
that gives you

00:00:19.823 --> 00:00:20.860
a slight difference in perspective,

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:22.550
'cause it's only an approximation,

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:23.890
of what your picture is gonna be.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:24.723
Now a way around

00:00:24.723 --> 00:00:26.390
that is by using an SLR camera,

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:28.560
which stands for single lens reflex.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:29.393
That's pretty much

00:00:29.393 --> 00:00:30.226
exactly what it is.

00:00:30.226 --> 00:00:31.059
There's only one lens,

00:00:31.059 --> 00:00:33.170
there's no separate viewfinder lens.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:34.003
Now that requires

00:00:34.003 --> 00:00:34.890
a little bit of cleverness

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:35.723
behind the lens,

00:00:35.723 --> 00:00:38.210
there's actually a mirror
placed at 45 degrees

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.730
that blocks the sensor,

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:41.020
which means you can look

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.840
through the viewfinder down

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:43.793
and through the exact lens

00:00:43.793 --> 00:00:45.090
it's gonna take the picture.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.230
So that leads us on to
the very first part.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.070
When you take a picture

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.870
you'll hear this sound,

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.020
didn't sound like much,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:51.853
but actually quite

00:00:51.853 --> 00:00:52.860
a lot just happen there.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:53.693
And you'll notice

00:00:53.693 --> 00:00:54.710
that in the viewfinder,

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:55.960
your vision was blocked

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.163
for a split second.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:00:59.790
So the only way we're gonna see

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:00.623
what actually happens

00:01:00.623 --> 00:01:02.310
is by taking off the lens,

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:03.770
zooming the Phantom in

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:04.870
and filming this

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.860
at a ridiculously high frame rate.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.230
We'll start with a 30th of a second.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:10.340
Now you're looking at the mirror

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.560
which is in front of the shutter.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:12.960
When I press down the button,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.030
the mirror flips up out

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:14.863
of the way

00:01:14.863 --> 00:01:16.280
and temporarily blocks the viewfinder,

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.650
then the shutter comes down, whoosh.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.450
Now light is exposing on the sensor

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.260
and then the shutter closes again,

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.220
followed by the mirror coming back down.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.053
So you can see

00:01:25.053 --> 00:01:25.886
through the viewfinder again.

00:01:25.886 --> 00:01:27.000
As you can see from that clip,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.480
this camera has a mechanical shutter

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.480
that moves out of the way

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.530
and allows light to hit the sensor.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.560
Very fundamental part of photography

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.570
is degeneration of your exposure

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.500
has very different effects on the image,

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.333
and you can control

00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:39.200
that with the shutter speed.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.360
For example, a relatively long exposure

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.683
of say two seconds,

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:46.930
will give you a bright image

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:47.880
but anything that moved

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.340
within those two seconds,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.173
will have a trail

00:01:50.173 --> 00:01:51.330
of motion blur behind it.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.770
So not ideal for filming
a fast moving subject.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:55.780
For a very fast moving subject,

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:56.613
you would want

00:01:56.613 --> 00:01:58.440
a much shorter shutter duration.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.730
The fastest this camera will do

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.000
it is in 8,000th of a second,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.000
which looks like this.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:04.920
So now what we'll do

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:05.980
is we'll use the Phantom

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.400
to film different shutter speeds

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.233
of the camera

00:02:08.233 --> 00:02:09.180
and see how they will compare

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.670
to each other in slow motion.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:11.590
Honestly, these are probably

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.230
gonna look identical in real time,

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:14.070
but we'll definitely be able

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:15.760
to see the difference in slow mo.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.593
So start with 500th of a second.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:24.883
1,000th of a second.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.743
A 2,000th of a second.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:37.943
4,000th of a second.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.403
and 8,000th of a second.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:50.213
Let's have a look

00:02:50.213 --> 00:02:51.046
at the four fastest ones

00:02:51.046 --> 00:02:51.879
side by side

00:02:51.879 --> 00:02:53.210
just to see the comparison.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.380
This kind of stuff is fascinating to me.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:25.810
It makes me wonder how many people,

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:26.643
take for granted

00:03:26.643 --> 00:03:27.940
what's happening inside the camera

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.050
when I take a picture.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:29.930
The fact that the shutter

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.860
is made of two clusters of metal blades

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:34.580
that at their shortest duration

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:35.990
are moving right next to each other

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:37.490
allowing just the smallest slit

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:39.220
of light to hit the sensor.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.210
This is also a great visual representation

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:42.560
of why you need so much light

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:44.090
for a high shutter speed shot.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.190
Because the amount of time

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:47.400
the light is hitting
the sensor is so short,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.200
you end up with a much darker image.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.360
There are many types of shutter

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.200
that a camera can have.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.920
This is a mechanical shutter,

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.380
but there's also an electronic shutter

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:55.780
which doesn't have any moving parts.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:56.860
In video mode,

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.070
this will actually use
an electronic shutter.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.530
It basically takes an image

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:01.860
30 times every second

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:03.340
without covering the sensor.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:04.280
This camera shutter

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:05.640
is also a rolling shutter,

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:06.520
which is the opposite

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:07.720
of a global shutter.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:08.560
And what that means is,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:09.393
is that it doesn't actually

00:04:09.393 --> 00:04:10.310
take the whole image

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.500
at the same time,

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.190
you may have noticed from the slow mo,

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:14.370
that the image is taken

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:15.670
from the top to the bottom

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:16.910
of the sensor.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:17.820
That basically means

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.600
that whenever you take a picture,

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:20.640
the top of the picture

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:21.700
is actually older

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:22.860
than the bottom

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:24.330
by a fraction of a second.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.420
And technically that statement

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.190
should be flipped as everything traveling

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:28.200
through the lens,

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.500
is actually upside down

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:31.030
by the time it gets to the sensor.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:32.910
The electronic shutter on this camera

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.280
when it's in video mode works

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:35.260
in the exact same way.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:36.830
It takes each line of pixels

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:37.810
from top to bottom

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:38.800
because it doesn't have the power

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.780
to take the whole image at once.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:41.613
And this can lead

00:04:41.613 --> 00:04:43.350
to some interesting artifacts

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.670
when filming certain subjects.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:46.620
You may remember this video

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:47.800
from a few years ago,

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.110
I was blasting Dan in
the face with a cork.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:51.840
And when I was editing the video,

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.480
I noticed something really weird

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:54.790
in the Canon footage.

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:56.540
On this one frame of video,

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.330
the cork is suspended in the air

00:04:58.330 --> 00:04:59.700
but the shadow of the cork

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.300
has already hit him in the face.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:03.470
Luckily we had the Phantom

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:04.370
right by its side

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:05.570
so we can analyze exactly

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:06.920
what happened with the canon there.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:09.070
So imagine the color
going down the screen,

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.700
as the canon acquiring

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.330
each frame of its video.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.330
Nothing happening in this frame,

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.290
watch this one though.

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:16.470
As it gets down to the cork firing

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.130
then hits the shadow.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:19.050
And that explains

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.560
why there are two different moments

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:22.670
in time at different heights

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:23.890
in the same frame.

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.310
Almost any camera you use

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:26.770
these days to shoot video,

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:28.130
will have a rolling shutter

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:29.500
and that's due to the frequency

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:32.280
of CMOS sensors being used in DSLRs

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:33.940
and pretty much every smartphone.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:34.890
And you can test this out

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:36.360
by pointing your camera outside

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.970
the window of a moving car,

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:39.620
and just filming trees and poles

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:40.453
that go by.

00:05:40.453 --> 00:05:41.890
You'll notice that
everything that's vertical,

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.350
will be slightly skewed diagonally

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:45.420
'cause the object is moving

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.190
as it's being scanned from top to bottom

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:48.423
or bottom to top.

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:52.580
There's definitely pros and cons

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:53.413
to both the global

00:05:53.413 --> 00:05:54.310
and the rolling shutter.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:55.950
For example, the Phantom flex

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.090
has a global shutter

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.240
which makes it much better

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.800
for the scientific analysis of shots,

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:02.240
as every moment in the frame

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:03.820
was taken at the exact same time.

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:06.740
A phantom flex 4k has a rolling shutter,

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.160
but it's typically used on movies

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.280
and TV where it seems

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:10.113
to be quite natural

00:06:10.113 --> 00:06:11.680
for the human eye to look at.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:12.620
Before I end this video,

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:14.000
I feel like I should say a few words

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.900
about the state of this camera.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.500
It's a Canon 7D,

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:18.420
I bought it right before

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:19.800
I started the Slow Mo Guys,

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:20.730
and I've used it

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.530
in every single episode

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:25.090
to film the real time segments.

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:26.730
And it's taken an absolute pounding.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.492
It's been in the splash zone a few times.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.810
Oh, it's right on the lens.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:41.410
It's a splurge

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:42.567
It's fallen over.

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:50.370
There's a screw loose,

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.070
I've just noticed,

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:53.350
but I refuse to replace it

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:54.350
because it still works.

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:55.970
So I hope you enjoyed that video,

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:57.200
maybe you learned something,

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.650
be sure to follow me on Twitter.

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.180
I tweet stuff,

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:01.090
you can buy a shirt,

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:02.113
you can subscribe.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:04.090
Thanks for watching.

